# Supabase RLS & Extensions — Migration Best Practices

## 1. Wrap `auth.*()` in `(select ...)`

In **every** RLS policy expression (`USING` / `WITH CHECK`), wrap `auth.uid()`, `auth.role()`, and `current_setting()` in a scalar sub-SELECT so Postgres evaluates the value once (initplan) instead of per-row.

```sql
-- ❌ BAD — re-evaluated for every row
using (auth.uid() = user_id)
using (exists (select 1 from profiles where id = auth.uid() and role = 'admin'))

-- ✅ GOOD — evaluated once as initplan
using ((select auth.uid()) = user_id)
using (exists (select 1 from profiles where id = (select auth.uid()) and role = 'admin'))
```

This applies to **all** policy types (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, ALL) on both `public.*` and `storage.objects` tables.

## 2. One permissive SELECT policy per table

Never create **two or more** `PERMISSIVE` policies for the **same action** (especially SELECT) on the same table — Postgres must evaluate all of them for every query, which is slower than a single policy with `OR`.

```sql
-- ❌ BAD — two permissive SELECT policies
create policy "Owners read own"  on t for select using ((select auth.uid()) = owner_id);
create policy "Admins read all"  on t for select using (is_admin());

-- ✅ GOOD — single policy with OR
create policy "Owners or admins read"
  on t for select
  using (
    (select auth.uid()) = owner_id
    or is_admin()
  );
```

When a table has an **admin `FOR ALL`** policy plus a **public/owner `FOR SELECT`** policy, split the admin `FOR ALL` into `FOR INSERT`, `FOR UPDATE`, `FOR DELETE` and merge the admin SELECT condition into the existing SELECT policy.

## 3. Extensions in `extensions` schema

Always install extensions in the `extensions` schema (not `public`):

```sql
-- ❌
create extension if not exists postgis;

-- ✅
create extension if not exists postgis schema extensions;
```

Functions that reference extension types (e.g. `geography`, `geometry`) must include `extensions` in their `search_path`:

```sql
set search_path = public, extensions
```
